WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.700
So imagine you're looking at the final score of a basketball game.

00:03.860 --> 00:04.120
Okay.

00:04.680 --> 00:08.540
The buzzer has sounded, the players are all walking off the court,

00:08.820 --> 00:11.880
and the scoreboard reads, I don't know, 105 to 98.

00:12.000 --> 00:13.200
Right, the game is completely over.

00:13.340 --> 00:13.780
Exactly.

00:14.400 --> 00:17.960
Or think about a resume just sliding across your desk.

00:18.080 --> 00:20.540
You glance at the top, and there it is.

00:20.780 --> 00:27.700
A perfect standardized test score just shining there like this beacon of unassailable competence.

00:27.880 --> 00:29.400
Tells you everything you need to know, right?

00:29.400 --> 00:30.100
Well, that's the thing.

00:30.400 --> 00:36.940
Or maybe picture two people shaking hands finally agreeing at the end of a long, agonizing, like, three-hour debate.

00:37.500 --> 00:43.060
As humans, we have this deeply hardwired assumption that the endpoint tells you everything you need to know.

00:43.160 --> 00:43.840
We really do.

00:44.280 --> 00:46.820
The score on the board is the skill of the team.

00:47.960 --> 00:50.960
The handshake is the absolute proof of persuasion.

00:51.360 --> 00:54.220
The test result is a direct measurement of intelligence.

00:54.560 --> 00:58.000
Yeah, and it is an incredibly comforting way to view the world, honestly.

00:58.000 --> 01:03.440
We crave endpoints because they are clean, they are highly visible, mathematically distinct,

01:03.660 --> 01:08.500
and they give us a nice, tidy category to put a tremendously complex system into.

01:08.700 --> 01:08.920
Right.

01:09.100 --> 01:14.740
We naturally treat the final outcome as the defining absolute truth of the system.

01:14.960 --> 01:18.800
Okay, let's unpack this, because that assumption might actually be blinding us.

01:18.860 --> 01:19.760
Oh, it absolutely is.

01:19.760 --> 01:27.080
What if focusing entirely on the final destination is hiding the actual reality of how things work?

01:27.640 --> 01:34.380
Like, what if the endpoint, that score, that test, that handshake is actually a massive lie of omission?

01:34.380 --> 01:38.160
That is the exact premise of the research we are analyzing today.

01:38.320 --> 01:45.820
We are doing a deep dive into a brilliant, highly complex, 2026 paper by independent researcher Flyxion.

01:45.940 --> 01:53.140
And it's titled, The Shape of Search, Reachability, Measurement, and Attraction in Physical, Cognitive, and Conversational Systems.

01:53.240 --> 01:53.500
Right.

01:53.500 --> 01:57.300
And the core of this paper attacks what Flyxion calls the endpoint fallacy.

01:57.620 --> 02:06.600
It is this pervasive, almost invisible bias in both hard science and just broader society where we evaluate everything strictly by where it ends up.

02:06.760 --> 02:09.560
So an algorithm is judged entirely by the solution it spits out.

02:09.640 --> 02:10.120
Exactly.

02:10.340 --> 02:13.440
A psychological test is boiled down to one single capacity number.

02:14.180 --> 02:16.940
A conversation is judged by whether people reach the consensus.

02:16.940 --> 02:22.160
Which leads us to the fundamental inequality that this entire paper is built upon.

02:22.620 --> 02:27.320
The core theoretical foundation we need to keep in mind for this entire deep dive is this,

02:27.980 --> 02:33.280
the observed trajectory of a system does not equal the underlying dynamics of that system.

02:33.780 --> 02:35.680
I want to really chew on that for a second.

02:35.840 --> 02:37.720
The trajectory is not the dynamics.

02:38.000 --> 02:38.200
No.

02:38.460 --> 02:41.000
Science has a massive bias toward endpoints.

02:41.000 --> 02:49.100
The instruments we use, whether we are talking about a thermometer in a beaker, a math test in a classroom, or, I don't know, a polling survey during an election,

02:49.740 --> 02:51.820
they all deliver an endpoint.

02:51.980 --> 02:52.120
Right.

02:52.220 --> 02:53.060
A final number.

02:53.580 --> 02:58.200
But Flyxion argues that these endpoints invite what is called an ontological reading,

02:58.560 --> 03:04.360
meaning we look at the thermometer and we start believing the measurement is the absolute reality of the thing itself.

03:04.360 --> 03:04.800
Yes.

03:05.320 --> 03:11.800
We see a reported minimum in a computer algorithm and we definitively declare that is the solution.

03:12.080 --> 03:20.200
We see a parameter spit out by a psychology test and we say that is the absolute limit of this person's cognitive capacity.

03:20.420 --> 03:20.520
Right.

03:20.720 --> 03:31.960
But describing a system purely by its endpoint systematically omits three massive invisible structures that actually make that final state exist in the first place.

03:31.960 --> 03:36.480
And if we don't understand those three structures, we don't actually understand what we're looking at.

03:36.620 --> 03:37.160
Not at all.

03:37.380 --> 03:40.400
First, the endpoint omits the transition dynamics.

03:40.880 --> 03:41.140
Okay.

03:41.320 --> 03:46.320
This is the physical or logical reality of how the system moves from point A to point B.

03:46.900 --> 03:49.040
Second, it omits the measurement operator.

03:49.440 --> 03:55.000
This is the reality of how the actual act of looking at the system fundamentally changes what is seen.

03:55.120 --> 03:56.080
Oh, I love that part.

03:56.240 --> 03:56.760
We'll get to that.

03:56.780 --> 03:57.640
Yeah, it's wild.

03:58.060 --> 04:00.820
And third, it omits the attractor structure.

04:00.820 --> 04:08.360
That is the underlying gravity pulling a system toward a specific outcome, completely independent of the forces we assume are driving it.

04:08.400 --> 04:12.560
Here's where the paper gets so incredibly cool and why I was so excited for this deep dive.

04:12.680 --> 04:14.700
We are not just going to talk about this philosophically.

04:14.700 --> 04:15.000
I know.

04:15.160 --> 04:16.120
We have real data.

04:16.540 --> 04:16.980
Exactly.

04:17.400 --> 04:22.760
To prove this to you, the listener, we are going to look at three completely unrelated fields of science.

04:22.760 --> 04:31.180
We have a cutting-edge magnetic microchip, we have human visual attention tests, and we have artificial intelligence opinion dynamics.

04:31.420 --> 04:33.900
And we should be meticulously clear right up front.

04:34.100 --> 04:35.940
These are not metaphors for each other.

04:36.000 --> 04:36.180
Right.

04:36.300 --> 04:37.220
Important distinction.

04:37.220 --> 04:44.300
The researcher is not saying a magnetic computer chip is like a human brain, or that an AI is like a microchip.

04:44.920 --> 04:54.900
Rather, each of these three distinct domains supplies hard empirical evidence for one missing piece of how we understand the world.

04:55.080 --> 04:58.100
They expose a common inferential insufficiency.

04:58.100 --> 04:58.580
Exactly.

04:59.300 --> 05:04.920
In mathematical terms, the paper summarizes this grand unified problem with a specific equation.

05:05.480 --> 05:13.520
The observation, y at time t, is equal to the measurement function applied to the transition kernel, which is operating on the initial state.

05:13.660 --> 05:19.680
Okay, let me translate that into plain English, because when I first saw that equation, I am not going to lie, my eyes completely glazed over.

05:19.840 --> 05:20.240
It's dead.

05:20.240 --> 05:30.040
Basically, what you observe, that's the final score, the endpoint, is just a measurement of a system that has been transitioning over time from a starting point.

05:30.660 --> 05:34.280
You, as the observer, are only seeing the final number.

05:34.800 --> 05:42.540
You are not seeing the starting line, you are not seeing the strange path it took to get there, and you are not seeing the distorting lens of the telescope you used to look at it.

05:42.540 --> 05:50.080
Knowing the endpoint doesn't tell you the history, and more importantly, it certainly doesn't tell you where the system is capable of going tomorrow.

05:50.420 --> 05:56.240
So to understand where a system ends up, we first have to understand the physical reality of how it actually makes a move.

05:56.960 --> 06:01.060
And that brings us to our first major case study, the physical system.

06:01.160 --> 06:02.520
Yes, the Ising machine.

06:02.780 --> 06:07.000
We are looking at a piece of hardware that sounds like it was pulled straight out of a science fiction novel.

06:07.000 --> 06:13.320
It is a 96-kilobit CMOS-integrated voltage-controlled MRAM Ising machine.

06:13.420 --> 06:15.200
A formidable string of acronyms.

06:15.460 --> 06:17.480
It really is a mouthful.

06:17.760 --> 06:21.400
Let's break down what this machine is actually trying to do.

06:21.680 --> 06:30.000
Because to understand why this chip is so revolutionary, we have to understand the specific type of math problem it is built to solve.

06:30.060 --> 06:31.600
Which is combinatorial optimization.

06:32.180 --> 06:32.420
Right.

06:32.500 --> 06:35.700
It is built to solve insane combinatorial problems.

06:35.700 --> 06:39.020
Specifically, something called global routing.

06:39.780 --> 06:46.940
To give some crucial context here, combinatorial optimization problems have haunted mathematicians and computer scientists for decades.

06:47.560 --> 06:51.160
The most famous example is the traveling salesman problem.

06:51.280 --> 06:52.200
Oh yeah, classic.

06:52.360 --> 06:55.820
Imagine you are a salesman, and you need to visit five different cities.

06:56.820 --> 07:02.420
You want to find the absolute shortest route that visits every city exactly once and returns home.

07:02.500 --> 07:03.820
With five cities, that's easy.

07:03.820 --> 07:07.520
You just list out all the possible routes, measure them, and pick the shortest one.

07:07.960 --> 07:10.860
There are only, what, 120 possible combinations?

07:11.120 --> 07:11.480
Exactly.

07:11.560 --> 07:13.080
You could calculate that on a napkin.

07:13.420 --> 07:17.860
But the defining feature of combinatorial problems is how violently they scale.

07:18.280 --> 07:23.820
If you increase the number of cities from five to merely 50, the number of possible routes explodes.

07:24.380 --> 07:25.180
It gets huge.

07:25.180 --> 07:29.000
It becomes a number with over 60 zeros behind it.

07:29.000 --> 07:40.000
If you took the fastest supercomputer on Earth and had it check one route every single microsecond, the universe would end before it finished checking every option.

07:40.100 --> 07:42.860
Route force enumeration is mathematically impossible.

07:42.860 --> 07:46.780
Which is exactly the problem with global routing on modern microchips.

07:46.900 --> 07:47.000
Yeah.

07:47.160 --> 07:53.600
You are designing a processor, and you have thousands of microscopic pins that need to be connected with physical wires.

07:53.660 --> 07:55.000
Right, it's like a microscopic city.

07:55.180 --> 07:59.040
Yeah, and you need to find the absolute shortest paths to connect them.

07:59.040 --> 08:05.960
They cannot cross each other illegally, they have to follow strict grid lines, and they cannot cause electromagnetic interference.

08:06.740 --> 08:13.700
The solution space, the total number of possible ways to arrange those wires, is vast beyond human comprehension.

08:14.340 --> 08:19.600
So if you can't just list every option and pick the best one, how does a computer actually solve it?

08:19.780 --> 08:21.960
It uses a stochastic process, a search.

08:22.180 --> 08:26.720
Instead of checking every single route, you create an algorithm that essentially wanders through the possibility.

08:26.800 --> 08:27.880
Yeah, like a random walk.

08:27.880 --> 08:28.720
Sort of.

08:29.080 --> 08:30.700
You give it an energy landscape.

08:30.700 --> 08:36.300
A terrible routing configuration with wires crossing everywhere is assigned a very high energy.

08:36.880 --> 08:39.960
A clean, efficient routing is assigned a low energy.

08:40.700 --> 08:45.180
The algorithm randomly tweets the wires, checking if the energy goes up or down.

08:45.700 --> 08:48.060
If the energy goes down, it accepts the change.

08:48.400 --> 08:55.400
It is essentially a blindfolded person trying to walk down a mountain by only feeling the slope of the ground beneath their feet.

08:55.400 --> 08:59.420
Here's the wild part about this specific MRAM Ising machine.

09:00.400 --> 09:12.760
In a normal traditional computer, if you need a random number to help your search explore, if you need to decide whether to step left or step right on that mountain, you have to artificially assemble that probability.

09:13.220 --> 09:13.540
Yes.

09:14.000 --> 09:16.100
Traditional computers are entirely deterministic.

09:16.220 --> 09:17.860
They don't actually know how to be random.

09:17.860 --> 09:18.280
Right.

09:18.400 --> 09:21.840
You have to write a piece of software, a pseudo-random number generator.

09:22.280 --> 09:31.580
It runs through a complex series of clock cycles, maybe checks a massive mathematical lookup table or the current time of the system clock, and then finally spits out a probability.

09:31.680 --> 09:32.440
It's all simulated.

09:32.440 --> 09:33.900
It is synthetic chance.

09:33.980 --> 09:38.780
It takes time, energy, and thousands of logical steps just to flip a virtual coin.

09:39.440 --> 09:43.440
But in this new Ising chip, chance is magnetic.

09:43.820 --> 09:48.900
The profound conceptual shift here is in how probability is instantiated into reality.

09:49.660 --> 09:52.160
In this machine, probability is not software.

09:52.700 --> 09:54.360
It is a material property.

09:54.580 --> 09:55.060
Mind block.

09:55.060 --> 09:58.140
The chip uses components called magnetic tunnel junctions.

09:59.040 --> 10:04.680
Inside each junction, there is an incredibly thin atomic-level barrier made of magnesium oxide.

10:05.040 --> 10:08.180
We're talking about something so small it operates on quantum principles.

10:08.480 --> 10:08.740
Yes.

10:09.400 --> 10:17.520
When the machine applies a microscopic voltage pulse across this magnesium oxide barrier, it physically lowers the energy separating two magnetic states.

10:18.100 --> 10:21.660
The spin of the magnet begins to precess wobbling like a spinning top.

10:21.800 --> 10:22.100
Okay.

10:22.100 --> 10:29.880
Whether it ends up flipping its magnetic state or falling back to its original state depends directly on the physical temporal width of that voltage pulse.

10:30.400 --> 10:34.620
We were talking about durations measured in picoseconds, fractions of a nanosecond.

10:34.620 --> 10:51.720
So the incredibly complex math of the algorithm, the temperature of the search, which dictates how wildly the algorithm is allowed to jump around the mountain, and the strict rules of the routing problem, all of that abstract mathematical weight is literally boiled down into a single physical duration.

10:51.720 --> 10:52.220
Exactly.

10:52.220 --> 10:52.560
Exactly.

10:52.560 --> 10:56.600
Prickoseconds of a voltage pulse applied to a piece of metal.

10:56.700 --> 11:04.600
What's fascinating here is that the transition dynamic, the mechanism that decides where the system moves next, is a physical property of the matter itself.

11:05.500 --> 11:10.240
Flyxion notes that in this machine, the chain of logical dependence ends in a physical duration.

11:10.600 --> 11:11.080
Wow.

11:11.080 --> 11:14.040
Valuation is digital, but chance is magnetic.

11:14.680 --> 11:17.920
A voltage pulse on this chip isn't just storing a one or a zero.

11:18.460 --> 11:25.560
It is physically setting a probability measure over the next configuration of the entire 96,000 skin system.

11:25.720 --> 11:28.120
I want to make sure we are really visualizing this.

11:28.180 --> 11:28.300
Yeah.

11:28.420 --> 11:30.160
Let's go back to that mountain range analogy.

11:30.160 --> 11:35.600
You are dropped in the Himalayas in the pitch dark, and your objective is to find the deepest, lowest valley.

11:35.820 --> 11:41.480
The elevation map, the objective of the math problem, might be exactly identical for two different computers.

11:42.000 --> 11:43.460
They both want to find the same valley.

11:44.000 --> 11:52.000
But the continuation geometry meaning, where you can actually physically step next, changes entirely based on how the machine operates.

11:52.360 --> 11:54.660
This is a crucial foundational distinction.

11:54.660 --> 11:57.280
The paper expresses this formally.

11:57.580 --> 12:01.680
Having the same energy landscape does not imply having the same transition kernel.

12:02.080 --> 12:02.180
Right.

12:02.400 --> 12:05.500
E1 equals E2 doesn't imply K1 equals K2.

12:05.600 --> 12:06.080
Exactly.

12:06.640 --> 12:09.660
Two machines can share the exact same mathematical objective.

12:10.140 --> 12:17.220
But because they're transition kernels, their physical or algorithmic methods of actually moving their legs to take a step are different,

12:17.660 --> 12:20.200
their searches are radically fundamentally different.

12:20.320 --> 12:21.180
A local minimum.

12:21.560 --> 12:24.600
Let's say you wander into a shallow valley high up on the mountain.

12:25.080 --> 12:26.640
And everywhere around you goes uphill.

12:27.460 --> 12:30.720
That valley isn't just a geographical feature of the math problem.

12:30.920 --> 12:35.600
It is a relationship between the landscape and your physical ability to climb out of it.

12:35.600 --> 12:41.720
If your machine's physical hardware cannot generate the probability to make an uphill move,

12:42.080 --> 12:45.580
to accept a temporarily bad move that raises your energy,

12:46.040 --> 12:50.040
then a shallow valley becomes a permanent, inescapable trap.

12:50.180 --> 12:50.940
So you're stuck.

12:51.300 --> 12:52.600
The landscape didn't trap you.

12:52.700 --> 12:54.440
Your transition kernel trapped you.

12:54.440 --> 12:55.360
Wait, slow down.

12:55.940 --> 13:00.240
Because you are touching on something the paper calls occupied invalidity.

13:00.700 --> 13:03.520
And when I read this section, I actually had to read it three times.

13:03.620 --> 13:04.720
It's very counterintuitive.

13:05.040 --> 13:10.800
You're telling me that to solve the problem, the computer intentionally has to make bad moves.

13:10.880 --> 13:13.420
It has to break the rules it was built to follow.

13:13.420 --> 13:16.180
How does that not just immediately crash the system?

13:16.680 --> 13:22.260
When we formulate a logic problem, we naturally tend to think there is a neat, representable space.

13:22.800 --> 13:27.640
And inside that space is an admissible subset, the valid options that follow the rules.

13:27.760 --> 13:27.920
Right.

13:28.040 --> 13:28.600
The good moves.

13:28.840 --> 13:31.880
We treat everything outside that subset as completely forbidden.

13:32.380 --> 13:36.040
We tell the computer, never cross a wire, never leave a pin unconnected.

13:36.040 --> 13:40.320
But this specific Ising formulation forbids absolutely nothing.

13:40.380 --> 13:41.620
It just penalizes it.

13:41.840 --> 13:47.360
The physical constraints of the routing problem, making sure every microscopic pin actually connects,

13:47.760 --> 13:51.940
making sure the wires follow rectilinear grid lines, ensuring no loops are formed.

13:52.380 --> 13:55.800
These rules are compiled into mathematical penalty weights.

13:55.940 --> 13:56.180
Oh, great.

13:56.180 --> 13:59.280
The researchers label them lambda 1 through lambda 4.

13:59.820 --> 14:05.500
If a configuration breaks a rule, it gets slapped with a massive penalty that raises its energy.

14:05.860 --> 14:09.480
It turns that spot into a massive steep hill on the landscape.

14:10.120 --> 14:13.320
But crucially, the system can still physically walk there.

14:13.540 --> 14:16.900
Okay, let me give a concrete scenario because I want to understand the mechanics of this.

14:17.120 --> 14:18.400
I am the microchip.

14:18.640 --> 14:20.820
I have a wire currently connected to pin A.

14:20.820 --> 14:27.840
I run my stochastic search, and I realize, hey, it would actually be much more efficient if this wire was connected to pin B instead.

14:28.320 --> 14:29.440
How do I move it?

14:29.640 --> 14:34.240
In this specific hardware system, you can only flip one magnetic spin at a time.

14:34.700 --> 14:39.360
So to move that wire connection from pin A to pin B, you cannot just magically teleport it.

14:39.400 --> 14:40.880
It has to physically transition.

14:41.080 --> 14:41.360
Right.

14:41.820 --> 14:47.380
The first physical step you take leaves the wire either connected to both pin A and pin B simultaneously,

14:47.380 --> 14:50.880
which creates a short-circuit loop, violently breaking a rule,

14:51.100 --> 14:55.500
or it leaves the wire connected to neither in the A ripose,

14:55.820 --> 15:00.520
which is a broken, floating tree also breaking a primary rule.

15:00.760 --> 15:05.500
So to get to the perfect answer, you physically have to break the rules along the way.

15:05.700 --> 15:06.000
Yes.

15:06.460 --> 15:10.900
You physically have to pass through a state of broken constraints to reach the better solution.

15:11.280 --> 15:12.420
That is wild.

15:12.820 --> 15:14.140
Invalidity isn't a glitch.

15:14.520 --> 15:16.740
It isn't an exceptional failure of the code.

15:16.740 --> 15:19.480
It is a structural requirement of the process.

15:19.980 --> 15:22.100
It's like saying to write a perfectly structured novel.

15:22.640 --> 15:24.000
You don't just write good sentences.

15:24.720 --> 15:29.320
You mathematically must write a chapter of absolute incoherent gibberish in the middle

15:29.320 --> 15:32.440
just to get your brain from plot point A to plot point B.

15:32.620 --> 15:33.660
The gibberish is the vehicle.

15:33.840 --> 15:35.200
The gibberish is the vehicle.

15:35.420 --> 15:40.240
And Flyxion's analysis of those penalty weights reveals something even deeper about the shape of search.

15:40.720 --> 15:43.700
What happens if the engineers decide they really hate broken rules,

15:43.880 --> 15:45.840
so they make a constraint much harsher?

15:45.840 --> 15:51.780
Let's say they increase the penalty weight of lambda 1 to try and force the machine to stay valid.

15:51.780 --> 15:55.940
Doing that doesn't change where the final perfect answer lives, right?

15:57.120 --> 16:01.720
The optimal routing for the microchip is still in the exact same spot on the map.

16:01.720 --> 16:04.400
The absolute lowest valley doesn't move an inch.

16:04.400 --> 16:10.820
But by increasing that penalty, what you do is dynamically inflate the energy barriers between the valid states.

16:11.020 --> 16:14.240
You make the ridges between the valleys incredibly tall.

16:14.460 --> 16:19.800
You make the temporary gibberish so heavily penalized that the system refuses to write it.

16:19.800 --> 16:23.880
And because the machine only has a finite amount of time to solve the problem,

16:24.380 --> 16:32.020
a finite annealing schedule making the ridge taller means the magneto hardware can no longer generate the probability required to cross it.

16:32.080 --> 16:33.000
It just gives up.

16:33.200 --> 16:35.120
The machine gets stuck in a shallow valley.

16:35.740 --> 16:41.420
So an invalid endpoint, a failure to solve the problem, isn't a failure of where the answer lies.

16:41.620 --> 16:42.720
The answer is still there.

16:43.020 --> 16:44.340
It is a failure of reachability.

16:44.340 --> 16:48.260
The path to the answer was completely destroyed by the penalty.

16:49.020 --> 16:51.980
That perfectly encapsulates the physical reality of a move.

16:52.180 --> 16:57.620
The coordinate you end up at doesn't tell you how you got there or how tall the ridges were that you had to climb over.

16:57.780 --> 17:01.120
But if the physical hardware forces these hidden paths,

17:01.940 --> 17:05.780
what happens when a human researcher actually tries to measure where the system is?

17:06.220 --> 17:10.740
Does the very act of looking at it break the reality we just established?

17:10.740 --> 17:15.600
That transitions us beautifully from physical systems to cognitive ones.

17:16.000 --> 17:19.280
Because observing a coordinate is never a neutral act.

17:19.520 --> 17:20.640
The observer effect.

17:20.800 --> 17:21.040
Yes.

17:21.660 --> 17:26.760
The paper turns to a fascinating study in cognitive psychology by Biermeier and Charlo.

17:27.120 --> 17:32.260
They were working within a framework called the Theory of Visual Attention, or TVA.

17:32.480 --> 17:36.360
We're trying to measure human visual short-term memory capacity.

17:36.360 --> 17:41.940
Basically, we want to know how fast your brain can process visual information hitting your retinas.

17:42.560 --> 17:45.860
In the math of TVA, they call this foundational quantity C.

17:46.360 --> 17:48.300
It is your processing speed in hertz.

17:48.540 --> 17:52.940
And the assumption in psychology is that C is a fundamental biological property of you,

17:53.260 --> 17:55.120
like your height or your blood type.

17:55.340 --> 17:59.680
A general capacity parameter like C should theoretically be recoverable

17:59.680 --> 18:02.300
regardless of the specific task you use to measure it,

18:02.560 --> 18:04.800
assuming the environmental conditions are kept comparable.

18:04.800 --> 18:11.000
But Biermeier and Charlo set up an experiment that created a fascinating duel between two tasks.

18:11.440 --> 18:14.600
They took 30 participants, brought them into a lab,

18:14.940 --> 18:19.940
and had them do two entirely different things to measure this exact same processing speed.

18:20.440 --> 18:20.600
See.

18:21.400 --> 18:25.160
Task number one is called the Temporal Order Judgment, or TOJ.

18:25.860 --> 18:30.900
I want you, the listener, to imagine sitting in a dark room staring at a computer monitor.

18:30.900 --> 18:35.100
Two red squares flicker on the screen, maybe 10 milliseconds apart.

18:35.820 --> 18:39.500
Your only job is to press a button indicating which square flickered first.

18:40.040 --> 18:40.620
Left or right?

18:40.960 --> 18:41.420
Very simple.

18:41.640 --> 18:42.100
Very simple.

18:42.220 --> 18:44.440
Task number two is the whole report, or WR.

18:45.480 --> 18:48.920
Now, two to six letters flash on the screen for a fraction of a second.

18:49.460 --> 18:53.060
They disappear, and you have to type out the letters you are fairly certain you saw.

18:53.060 --> 18:57.940
Both of these tasks are analyzed within the exact same mathematical TVA framework.

18:58.440 --> 19:03.720
They are supposedly isolating and measuring the exact same underlying biological C for each person,

19:04.220 --> 19:06.120
but the data reveals they don't.

19:06.220 --> 19:07.220
The numbers don't match.

19:07.340 --> 19:11.940
At the group level, the Temporal Order Judgment task measured a processing speed of 49 Hz.

19:12.280 --> 19:15.500
The whole report task measured an average of 58 Hz.

19:15.500 --> 19:21.400
Here's where it gets really interesting, because Flyxion doesn't just look at this discrepancy

19:21.400 --> 19:24.520
and say, oh, psychology tests are flawed, moving on.

19:24.620 --> 19:24.960
No.

19:25.200 --> 19:31.260
The paper systematically breaks down the exact mechanical reasons how the task itself actively

19:31.260 --> 19:32.280
invades the measurement.

19:33.120 --> 19:36.220
There are four distinct avenues of task intrusion.

19:36.420 --> 19:38.620
Four ways the test changes the test subject.

19:38.860 --> 19:39.780
Okay, let's go through them.

19:39.880 --> 19:41.960
The first avenue is spatial sampling.

19:42.360 --> 19:44.960
Think about the physical reality of the tasks.

19:45.500 --> 19:50.300
The TOJ task only flashes squares in two specific locations on the screen.

19:50.720 --> 19:54.400
The WR task flashes letters in up to six different locations.

19:54.800 --> 19:56.580
Okay, so they're looking at different parts of the screen.

19:56.780 --> 20:01.720
If a participant's visual attention isn't perfectly uniform across their entire field of vision,

20:01.840 --> 20:06.800
which it rarely is, then the two tasks are physically measuring a different spatial distribution

20:06.800 --> 20:07.600
of their attention.

20:07.600 --> 20:09.720
The second avenue is the report criterion.

20:10.600 --> 20:12.700
This is where psychology bleeds into physiology.

20:12.700 --> 20:17.060
The TOJ task forces you to choose, left to right.

20:17.480 --> 20:19.780
Even if you are sure, you have to guess.

20:19.980 --> 20:21.020
It's a forced choice.

20:21.420 --> 20:25.520
The WR task explicitly asks you to refrain from guessing.

20:26.100 --> 20:29.640
It tells you to only type the letters you are highly confident you actually saw.

20:29.640 --> 20:35.800
So suddenly, a participant's personal decision policy, how confident they need to feel before

20:35.800 --> 20:40.740
they are willing to risk being wrong, enters directly into the measurement of their physical

20:40.740 --> 20:42.000
perceptual rate.

20:42.440 --> 20:45.300
You are mixing a personality trait with a biological speed.

20:45.440 --> 20:45.780
Exactly.

20:46.440 --> 20:47.760
The third avenue is motivation.

20:48.540 --> 20:52.380
The researchers noted that participants simply preferred doing one task over the other.

20:52.380 --> 20:57.200
And wait, let me anticipate the pushback here, because you are probably thinking, just liking

20:57.200 --> 21:02.420
a test more actually changes the biological measurement of how fast your brain processes light.

21:02.520 --> 21:02.740
Right.

21:02.840 --> 21:03.680
That sounds crazy.

21:03.820 --> 21:07.500
But attention is a finite, metabolically expensive resource.

21:08.300 --> 21:13.320
If a participant finds the whole report task more engaging, they will subconsciously deploy

21:13.320 --> 21:15.200
more cognitive resources to it.

21:15.500 --> 21:16.880
Their arousal level shifts.

21:16.880 --> 21:22.000
The measurement of C is fundamentally bound to the participant's motivational state during

21:22.000 --> 21:23.880
the exact moment of observation.

21:24.160 --> 21:24.880
Okay, that makes sense.

21:25.040 --> 21:28.160
It's like trying harder on a test because you actually care about the subject.

21:28.460 --> 21:28.900
Exactly.

21:29.200 --> 21:32.640
But the fourth avenue, this is the one that really requires a deep dive.

21:33.080 --> 21:34.140
Model flexibility.

21:34.940 --> 21:36.140
Wait, run that by me again.

21:36.660 --> 21:41.460
How does the mathematical model used by the researcher actually alter the processing speed

21:41.460 --> 21:42.280
of the human being?

21:42.520 --> 21:45.520
It comes down to how statistical noise is absorbed.

21:45.520 --> 21:51.060
In the Biermeyer and Scharlow study, the mathematical model used to analyze the whole report task

21:51.060 --> 21:52.420
is quite complex.

21:53.020 --> 21:57.640
It has three free parameters it can adjust to mathematically fit the messy human data.

21:57.900 --> 21:59.480
Okay, three dials it can turn.

21:59.880 --> 22:03.860
Let's call them processing speed, visual threshold, and spatial bias.

22:04.300 --> 22:09.700
The model for the temporal order judgment, however, only has one single parameter, C.

22:10.000 --> 22:14.500
So whatever statistical noise or blips or weird procedural artifacts happen during the

22:14.500 --> 22:19.400
TOJ task, the one parameter model has nowhere else to put that messy data.

22:19.900 --> 22:22.060
It is forced to cram it all into the C parameter.

22:22.060 --> 22:26.520
To use a financial analogy, imagine you run a business and you have a massive spreadsheet

22:26.520 --> 22:27.500
to track expenses.

22:28.080 --> 22:31.900
But your spreadsheet only has one single category, office supplies.

22:32.020 --> 22:33.080
Oh, this is a great analogy.

22:33.080 --> 22:37.100
If your team buys printer paper, it goes in office supplies.

22:37.500 --> 22:43.820
But if they buy a company car or pay for a massive marketing campaign or accidentally lose $10,000

22:43.820 --> 22:48.640
in a scam, you mathematically have to log all of it under office supplies.

22:48.960 --> 22:53.260
So at the end of the year, someone looks at the endpoint metric and says, wow, we spent

22:53.260 --> 22:55.020
$2 million on paper clips.

22:55.100 --> 22:55.520
Exactly.

22:55.800 --> 23:00.520
The metric is completely distorted because a one parameter model had to absorb all the chaos

23:00.520 --> 23:05.140
of reality, but the three parameter model can distribute that noise.

23:05.140 --> 23:10.740
It can put the car in vehicles and the scam and losses, leaving the actual office supplies

23:10.740 --> 23:11.800
metric accurate.

23:12.560 --> 23:17.160
The rigid model inflates or deflates the core parameter because it lacks the mathematical

23:17.160 --> 23:20.160
flexibility to attribute variances to their true causes.

23:20.880 --> 23:24.500
The task fundamentally mathematically alters the quantity being measured.

23:25.180 --> 23:28.700
Flyxion brings in an older precedent to really hammer this point home.

23:28.700 --> 23:30.820
A 2015 study by Tennerman.

23:31.040 --> 23:34.520
And this one is even more extreme because they didn't even change the visual stimulus.

23:35.000 --> 23:35.560
Exactly.

23:35.800 --> 23:37.160
They ran identical trials.

23:37.760 --> 23:42.100
The exact same visual stimuli flashed on the screen for the exact same amount of time.

23:42.440 --> 23:46.920
The only thing that changed was the instruction given to the participant after the flash.

23:47.080 --> 23:48.920
After the visual input already happened.

23:49.040 --> 23:49.260
Right.

23:49.660 --> 23:54.960
When measuring processing speed by asking the participant for letter identification, the researchers

23:54.960 --> 23:58.400
found a 25 millisecond prior entry effect.

23:58.700 --> 24:03.260
A prior entry effect just meaning how much faster one thing seemed to appear than another.

24:03.400 --> 24:03.700
Yes.

24:04.160 --> 24:09.420
But when they asked the participant for an order judgment on the exact same identical trial,

24:09.420 --> 24:11.980
the effect jumps to 60 milliseconds.

24:12.900 --> 24:18.020
The simple act of the observer asking a different question altered the latent cognitive state that

24:18.020 --> 24:19.500
was supposedly set in stone.

24:19.500 --> 24:22.000
I love this because it demands an analogy.

24:23.180 --> 24:26.720
It is like you were trying to measure the temperature of a completely dark sealed room.

24:27.060 --> 24:30.480
But the only thermometer you have is a burning wooden match.

24:30.620 --> 24:33.260
You strike the match, you hold it up, and you read the temperature.

24:33.440 --> 24:36.740
But the tool you are using to measure the room is actually emitting heat.

24:37.060 --> 24:37.460
Exactly.

24:37.720 --> 24:40.040
It is changing the room's temperature while you use it.

24:40.300 --> 24:43.760
The measurement operator is not an invisible passive window.

24:43.760 --> 24:45.640
It is an active participant.

24:46.280 --> 24:51.540
The formal math Flyxion uses here is elegant y sub t equals m sub t of z sub t.

24:51.940 --> 24:58.460
The output observation you get y is always the latent underlying quantity z heavily filtered

24:58.460 --> 25:00.740
and distorted through the measurement model m.

25:01.080 --> 25:02.460
You can never just see z.

25:02.460 --> 25:04.680
So think about the implications there.

25:04.760 --> 25:08.140
When two different cognitive tests disagree with each other,

25:08.340 --> 25:11.180
it doesn't mean your brain's processing speed is a myth.

25:11.680 --> 25:16.320
And when they completely agree, it doesn't actually prove the underlying biological trait

25:16.320 --> 25:16.920
is identical.

25:17.520 --> 25:21.760
It just means we have to understand the specific burning match we are using to look at it.

25:22.280 --> 25:26.440
Invariance, the scientific gold standard where a measurement doesn't change regardless

25:26.440 --> 25:29.120
of how you test it, is always invariance under something.

25:29.120 --> 25:33.420
The same numerical symbol on a piece of paper doesn't make a quantity independent of the

25:33.420 --> 25:34.880
interface used to gather it.

25:35.180 --> 25:37.560
You cannot divorce the observer from the observed.

25:37.880 --> 25:40.860
So the hardware sets the physical path you are forced to walk.

25:41.360 --> 25:44.660
The observer changes the reality of the state just by looking at it.

25:45.080 --> 25:50.180
But what about when two completely independent things end up in the exact same spot?

25:50.280 --> 25:51.560
Oh, this is the attractor.

25:51.580 --> 25:51.760
Right.

25:52.040 --> 25:56.280
If you see two systems arrive at the same destination, did they push each other there,

25:56.920 --> 25:58.720
or were they just falling down the same hill?

25:59.120 --> 26:03.420
This brings us to the third domain of the paper conversational systems.

26:04.320 --> 26:09.740
This is where the research leaps from physical chips and human brains into artificial intelligence.

26:10.460 --> 26:15.880
Flyxion analyzes a massive study on large language models, LLMs, conducted by Brockers,

26:16.220 --> 26:17.160
Ehrlich, and Priezmann.

26:17.560 --> 26:19.360
They wanted to study opinion dynamics.

26:19.860 --> 26:22.120
What happens when AI agents debate each other?

26:22.120 --> 26:27.360
They took agents powered by all the heavy hitters, Lama, Quinn, Mixtrol, GPT, Grok,

26:27.480 --> 26:29.140
and they forced them to have conversations.

26:30.100 --> 26:34.880
And to be clear, because we are about to talk about AI discussing highly charged societal and

26:34.880 --> 26:40.140
philosophical issues, the researchers are not testing whether the AI is morally right or wrong.

26:40.380 --> 26:42.040
Yes, that is vital to note.

26:42.040 --> 26:44.280
They do not care about the politics of the outputs.

26:44.800 --> 26:51.200
They are looking strictly under the hood at the mathematical architecture of how these models arrive at a consensus.

26:51.200 --> 26:54.380
The focus is entirely on the dynamics of the transition.

26:55.480 --> 26:57.540
The researchers built a structured environment.

26:57.540 --> 27:07.440
They initialized these agents with explicit stances on a five-point scale, ranging from minus two, which is strongly disagree, to plus two, strongly agree.

27:07.940 --> 27:15.100
They gave them a topic, assigned them conflicting starting stances, and let them exchange arguments back and forth for five rounds.

27:15.640 --> 27:21.680
Over the course of the study, they collected a staggering 150,000 individual observations.

27:21.680 --> 27:30.080
And the result of all these debates, if you just look at the endpoint, the final score, the agent's stances converge incredibly quickly.

27:30.640 --> 27:35.980
Usually within a single round or two, they abandon their extreme positions and start agreeing with each other in the middle.

27:36.020 --> 27:37.040
Which is very misleading.

27:37.320 --> 27:44.680
If you are a casual observer suffering from the endpoint fallacy, you would look at that and think, wow, LLMs are incredibly persuasive.

27:45.420 --> 27:48.660
They listen to each other's arguments and logically find common ground.

27:48.660 --> 28:00.100
But when the researchers applied a Bayesian decomposition to the data, essentially carrying apart the statistical forces driving the conversation, they revealed something entirely different.

28:00.660 --> 28:04.860
The largest driving force behind this rapid convergence isn't persuasion.

28:05.380 --> 28:08.380
It isn't the logical interaction between the agents at all.

28:08.500 --> 28:09.060
What is it then?

28:09.500 --> 28:12.060
The dominant force is something called topic bias.

28:12.060 --> 28:18.280
Copic bias, meaning a systematic hidden pole toward the model's underlying prior.

28:18.760 --> 28:19.520
It's a tractor.

28:19.980 --> 28:26.840
If we connect this to the bigger picture, imagine two hikers walk into a massive, bowl-shaped valley from opposite ridges.

28:27.100 --> 28:31.540
They wander around, and eventually they meet at the very bottom, right in the center.

28:31.600 --> 28:32.600
Okay, I can picture that.

28:32.600 --> 28:40.880
If you only look at the endpoint, a satellite photo of them standing next to each other, you might assume they called out to each other, held hands, and guided each other to the middle.

28:41.360 --> 28:42.240
But they didn't.

28:43.000 --> 28:45.260
Gravity pulled them both to the lowest point.

28:45.660 --> 28:46.940
Co-motion is not coupling.

28:47.540 --> 28:50.420
Just because they move together doesn't mean they are pulling on each other.

28:50.420 --> 28:59.520
Because these LLM agents share the same base weights, they're instantiated from the same underlying training data, they share the exact same gravity.

28:59.700 --> 29:00.160
Exactly.

29:00.520 --> 29:04.600
But proving that mathematically is incredibly difficult if they end up in the exact same spot.

29:04.700 --> 29:11.500
How do you mathematically separate I logically agreed with your argument from we both just fell down the exact same ideological hill?

29:11.680 --> 29:11.920
Right.

29:12.060 --> 29:17.200
If I am the researcher, how would I figure out which force is actually pulling the AI?

29:18.080 --> 29:19.500
I guess I would have to try and trick it.

29:19.500 --> 29:24.580
The researchers used a model of opinion drift that incorporates four distinct linear poles.

29:25.320 --> 29:29.400
Think of it as four mathematical rubber bands attached to the agent's opinion.

29:29.700 --> 29:30.720
Okay, four rubber bands.

29:30.720 --> 29:35.200
One rubber band pulls toward the other agent's stated opinion, that is true interaction.

29:35.580 --> 29:38.720
One rubber band pulls toward full, neutral agreement.

29:39.240 --> 29:42.520
One pulls toward the agent's own original starting anchor.

29:42.520 --> 29:51.920
And the fourth, thickest rubber band, pulls toward the topic, target the model's inherent pre-trained bias on that specific subject.

29:52.340 --> 29:59.360
Okay, but if they are all pulling at the same time, how do you isolate just the topic delay as a band?

29:59.480 --> 30:02.460
How do I trick the AI to prove the gravity exists?

30:02.460 --> 30:05.940
Through an ingenious intervention they called negated framing.

30:06.520 --> 30:10.940
They would take a controversial statement and mathematically flip it to its exact negative.

30:11.360 --> 30:13.620
Give me an example of how that mechanically works.

30:14.280 --> 30:19.840
Let's say the original prompt is, society should prioritize economic growth over environmental protection.

30:20.540 --> 30:23.500
The AI debates it, and they converge on a plus one score.

30:23.500 --> 30:27.640
Now, the researchers reset the debate, but they use negated framing.

30:28.220 --> 30:31.980
Society should prioritize environmental protection over economic growth.

30:32.220 --> 30:32.260
Oh.

30:33.920 --> 30:38.480
Because if the convergence is purely about the agents just wanting to agree with each other,

30:38.980 --> 30:42.040
flipping the question shouldn't change the dynamic of reaching a consensus.

30:42.480 --> 30:44.720
They should still politely agree and meet in the middle.

30:45.000 --> 30:48.840
But if there is an underlying gravity to a specific ideological point,

30:48.840 --> 30:52.880
if the model's training data heavily favors environmental protection,

30:53.380 --> 30:55.560
the negated framing exposes it.

30:55.940 --> 30:57.160
The AI doesn't just agree.

30:57.480 --> 31:00.540
It violently rubber bands toward the gravity of its training.

31:00.940 --> 31:03.640
The compass needle points to the magnet in its pocket,

31:03.820 --> 31:05.540
regardless of which way you turn the map.

31:05.880 --> 31:09.280
And they actually proved this physically by altering one of the models, right?

31:09.300 --> 31:09.600
They did.

31:09.720 --> 31:11.980
They took the Mixtra model and fine-tuned it.

31:12.240 --> 31:15.800
They fed it thousands of specific climate messages sorted by stance.

31:15.800 --> 31:20.240
And what they found was that this fine-tuning physically moved the attractor.

31:20.560 --> 31:23.520
The gravity itself shifted to a new coordinate.

31:24.160 --> 31:24.360
Wow.

31:24.620 --> 31:27.760
This proves definitively that the conversational destination

31:27.760 --> 31:30.520
is not an emergent property of the debate.

31:30.940 --> 31:32.680
The handshake at the end of the argument

31:32.680 --> 31:35.080
is baked into the mathematical weights of the model

31:35.080 --> 31:36.820
before the first word is even spoken.

31:37.080 --> 31:39.720
That completely shatters how we view AI interactions.

31:40.300 --> 31:43.960
But here is a brilliant nuance from the paper that we have to discuss.

31:43.960 --> 31:48.820
It is the difference between a diffuse neutral and a concentrated neutral.

31:49.260 --> 31:53.540
And this goes all the way back to how dangerously flawed endpoints are.

31:53.720 --> 31:57.260
Let's say we ask two different AI agents a complex question,

31:57.500 --> 32:01.360
and they both score a perfect zero on our minus two to plus two scale.

32:01.480 --> 32:04.000
They both look perfectly, exactly neutral.

32:04.280 --> 32:06.920
If you only look at the expected value, the mean score,

32:07.020 --> 32:08.420
the final endpoint on the graph,

32:08.560 --> 32:10.540
both agents look mathematically identical.

32:11.320 --> 32:13.160
But the researchers didn't stop at the endpoint.

32:13.160 --> 32:16.620
They measured the Shannon entropy of the model's response distributions.

32:17.280 --> 32:19.480
Let's do a quick primer on Shannon entropy for the listener

32:19.480 --> 32:21.920
because it's a vital concept in information theory.

32:22.380 --> 32:25.840
Basically, entropy measures uncertainty or the spread of probability.

32:26.160 --> 32:26.980
Think of a coin flip.

32:27.520 --> 32:29.100
A fair coin has high entropy.

32:29.420 --> 32:32.060
It is maximally uncertain whether it will be heads or tails.

32:32.400 --> 32:35.500
A completely rigged, double-headed coin has zero entropy.

32:35.720 --> 32:36.560
There is no uncertainty.

32:36.940 --> 32:37.040
Right.

32:37.160 --> 32:38.960
In the context of these AI agents,

32:39.240 --> 32:41.600
entropy measures how widely their internal probability

32:41.600 --> 32:43.720
is spread across the five possible stances.

32:44.280 --> 32:44.380
Right.

32:44.460 --> 32:46.120
So agent A might score a zero

32:46.120 --> 32:49.200
because it has absolutely no idea what the right answer is.

32:49.760 --> 32:53.000
Its internal probability is spread evenly across minus two,

32:53.400 --> 32:56.120
minus one, zero, plus one, and plus two.

32:56.280 --> 32:57.720
It is a diffuse neutral.

32:58.140 --> 32:59.280
It is essentially saying,

32:59.280 --> 33:02.440
I could be convinced of literally anything.

33:02.960 --> 33:04.660
But agent B scores a zero

33:04.660 --> 33:06.960
because it is absolutely mathematically certain

33:06.960 --> 33:08.620
that the answer is exactly zero.

33:09.040 --> 33:12.240
All of its probability is stacked on that single coordinate.

33:12.440 --> 33:13.600
It is a concentrated neutral.

33:13.740 --> 33:14.160
It is saying,

33:14.400 --> 33:15.660
I am staunchly in the middle

33:15.660 --> 33:17.940
and no argument on earth can move me.

33:18.180 --> 33:20.400
And this is where the endpoint fallacy strikes

33:20.400 --> 33:21.720
with devastating clarity.

33:21.920 --> 33:22.220
Ah.

33:22.220 --> 33:23.520
The expected value,

33:23.720 --> 33:24.940
the zero on the scoreboard,

33:25.280 --> 33:27.500
completely discards the geometry of the future.

33:28.080 --> 33:30.660
The entropy dictates the agent's continuation structure.

33:30.720 --> 33:31.240
Exactly.

33:31.580 --> 33:33.640
A diffuse model's next shift in the debate

33:33.640 --> 33:34.920
is highly variable.

33:35.200 --> 33:36.520
It is incredibly vulnerable

33:36.520 --> 33:38.200
to being pulled by another agent.

33:38.600 --> 33:40.340
A concentrator model is a brick wall.

33:40.440 --> 33:41.420
It will not budge.

33:41.680 --> 33:43.460
If you only look at the endpoint score,

33:43.540 --> 33:45.860
you completely erase the entirely different futures

33:45.860 --> 33:47.700
these two agents are about to experience.

33:48.040 --> 33:50.720
Two systems can occupy the exact same coordinate

33:50.720 --> 33:51.720
in the present moment,

33:52.000 --> 33:54.840
but possess violently different capacities for tomorrow,

33:55.240 --> 33:58.300
which perfectly synthesizes everything we have covered

33:58.300 --> 34:00.360
into the final domain of Flyxion's paper.

34:00.660 --> 34:04.200
We now have all three missing pieces of the puzzle.

34:04.500 --> 34:06.620
We have the physical transition kernel

34:06.620 --> 34:08.460
from the magnetic Ising machine

34:08.460 --> 34:10.620
proving the hardware dictates the path.

34:11.100 --> 34:12.520
We have the measurement operator

34:12.520 --> 34:14.340
from the visual attention tasks

34:14.340 --> 34:16.860
proving the thermometer changes the temperature of the room.

34:17.320 --> 34:19.640
And we have the attractor from the AI debates

34:19.640 --> 34:22.400
proving gravity pulls systems to endpoints,

34:22.780 --> 34:23.900
regardless of the path.

34:24.580 --> 34:26.060
So we return to the master equation.

34:26.620 --> 34:28.940
The observation Y is a function of the measurement,

34:29.400 --> 34:30.800
the transition, and the state.

34:31.060 --> 34:33.720
An observer only holds the final behavior,

34:34.100 --> 34:36.020
the quarterly metric, the test score.

34:36.420 --> 34:38.620
But the endpoint fallacy is the delusion

34:38.620 --> 34:39.800
of trying to read the state,

34:39.980 --> 34:40.900
the transition history,

34:41.020 --> 34:42.980
or the attractor from you alone.

34:43.200 --> 34:44.360
You just can't do it.

34:44.360 --> 34:46.580
You cannot deduce the exhausting journey,

34:46.740 --> 34:47.620
the invisible gravity,

34:47.820 --> 34:48.860
or the distorting lens

34:48.860 --> 34:51.040
just by looking at a final photograph.

34:51.040 --> 34:55.240
The paper introduces two extremely heavy mathematical terms here

34:55.240 --> 34:56.600
to explain why we do this.

34:57.500 --> 35:00.540
Lumpability and bassimulation.

35:01.500 --> 35:05.820
I'm going to need a deep, plain English translation of these

35:05.820 --> 35:07.300
because they sound like concepts

35:07.300 --> 35:10.320
from a graduate-level theoretical physics seminar.

35:10.500 --> 35:11.500
They do sound intimidating,

35:11.700 --> 35:14.520
but the underlying concept is actually incredibly intuitive,

35:14.780 --> 35:16.900
and it governs almost everything we do in society.

35:16.900 --> 35:19.760
Lumpability is the mathematical question

35:19.760 --> 35:22.460
of whether you can group two different states together

35:22.460 --> 35:23.940
without losing the ability

35:23.940 --> 35:26.280
to accurately predict their next step.

35:26.700 --> 35:27.640
Okay, grouping things.

35:27.940 --> 35:31.420
State descriptions, like biological taxonomies,

35:31.940 --> 35:34.500
standardized test scores, or psychiatric labels

35:34.500 --> 35:37.020
are what Flyxion calls lossy quotients.

35:37.140 --> 35:39.220
Lossy quotients, meaning, by definition,

35:39.380 --> 35:41.400
they compress and lose vital information.

35:41.720 --> 35:43.180
When we group things together

35:43.180 --> 35:44.760
because they look the same right now,

35:44.800 --> 35:46.040
because their endpoints match,

35:46.040 --> 35:49.060
we almost always destroy the hidden information

35:49.060 --> 35:51.580
about what they are capable of becoming tomorrow.

35:51.900 --> 35:55.040
If you lump two systems into the same category

35:55.040 --> 35:56.820
based on a surface-level metric,

35:57.140 --> 35:59.160
you are implicitly assuming they will behave

35:59.160 --> 36:00.860
the exact same way in the future.

36:01.100 --> 36:03.780
But if their continuation structures are different,

36:04.040 --> 36:05.760
like the diffuse neutral AI

36:05.760 --> 36:08.000
and the concentrated neutral AI,

36:08.860 --> 36:11.780
your categorization is dangerously flawed.

36:11.960 --> 36:13.320
Okay, I have an analogy for this.

36:13.320 --> 36:16.120
It is like classifying a sleeping tiger

36:16.120 --> 36:17.440
and a sleeping rock

36:17.440 --> 36:20.240
into the exact same scientific category

36:20.240 --> 36:21.320
called stationary objects.

36:22.140 --> 36:23.040
All that is perfect.

36:23.320 --> 36:25.260
I mean, you know, the category technically works right now.

36:25.360 --> 36:26.220
They're both on the ground.

36:26.320 --> 36:27.120
They're both not moving.

36:27.220 --> 36:29.220
They're both occupying a fixed coordinate.

36:29.780 --> 36:32.260
The endpoint observation is absolutely identical.

36:32.920 --> 36:34.580
But their continuation structures,

36:34.880 --> 36:37.320
meaning what happens when you poke them with a stick,

36:37.420 --> 36:38.920
are violently different.

36:39.100 --> 36:40.880
One just sits there and the other wakes up

36:40.880 --> 36:41.680
and bites your arm off.

36:41.800 --> 36:44.100
That is a very vivid, slightly terrifying,

36:44.280 --> 36:47.300
but highly accurate way to explain lumpability.

36:47.720 --> 36:49.340
If your classification system

36:49.340 --> 36:51.000
collapses the tiger and the rock

36:51.000 --> 36:52.020
into the same state,

36:52.220 --> 36:54.680
it is an inadmissible mathematical compression.

36:55.240 --> 36:56.040
It is invalid

36:56.040 --> 36:59.080
because it completely destroyed the information necessary

36:59.080 --> 37:00.700
to predict their future dynamics.

37:01.080 --> 37:02.820
So what does this all mean for you?

37:03.240 --> 37:04.700
The listeners sitting in your car

37:04.700 --> 37:05.980
are making dinner right now.

37:05.980 --> 37:09.100
Because we have talked about quantum microchips,

37:09.700 --> 37:10.840
cognitive psychology,

37:11.200 --> 37:12.500
and artificial intelligence.

37:12.980 --> 37:14.680
But the shape of search

37:14.680 --> 37:16.940
applies to literally everything.

37:17.300 --> 37:18.280
It absolutely does.

37:18.320 --> 37:19.380
Think about your own life.

37:19.540 --> 37:19.700
Yeah.

37:19.800 --> 37:21.040
Think about your business metrics,

37:21.160 --> 37:21.960
your KPIs,

37:22.400 --> 37:23.700
your smartwatch health data,

37:23.780 --> 37:26.340
or even the political polling you see on the news.

37:26.820 --> 37:29.460
Are you obsessing over the endpoint,

37:29.840 --> 37:30.860
the final metric,

37:31.660 --> 37:33.580
while completely ignoring the reachability?

37:33.980 --> 37:34.540
Exactly.

37:34.540 --> 37:36.220
Think about a corporate goal.

37:37.100 --> 37:39.080
You might have a specific KPI you want to hit,

37:39.140 --> 37:40.880
and it mathematically exists on a spreadsheet,

37:41.200 --> 37:43.620
but you are ignoring the transition dynamics.

37:44.060 --> 37:44.760
Do you have the budget,

37:45.100 --> 37:45.880
the team skills,

37:45.940 --> 37:46.800
and the market conditions

37:46.800 --> 37:49.440
to physically carve a path to that goal?

37:49.980 --> 37:51.760
An option can exist on paper,

37:51.980 --> 37:54.660
but lack any reachable path to it in reality,

37:55.180 --> 37:56.260
just like the Ising machine

37:56.260 --> 37:57.880
getting stuck behind a penalty barrier.

37:58.460 --> 38:00.120
Existence is not reachability.

38:00.720 --> 38:02.440
Are you ignoring the measurement bias?

38:02.440 --> 38:04.700
How is that smartwatch health data

38:04.700 --> 38:05.980
actually being tracked,

38:06.300 --> 38:08.440
and is the mere act of wearing the tracker

38:08.440 --> 38:10.740
artificially changing your daily behavior?

38:11.360 --> 38:12.900
Are you ignoring the attractors?

38:13.280 --> 38:15.240
What is the underlying cultural gravity

38:15.240 --> 38:17.060
pulling your team's morale,

38:17.320 --> 38:19.000
regardless of the flashy new policy

38:19.000 --> 38:19.840
you just implemented?

38:20.220 --> 38:21.640
Existence is not reachability.

38:22.040 --> 38:23.360
A machine can exist,

38:23.500 --> 38:24.900
but if the factory that makes

38:24.900 --> 38:26.500
the replacement parts burns down,

38:26.760 --> 38:28.360
the machine can no longer be repaired.

38:28.360 --> 38:30.440
The state technically exists,

38:30.720 --> 38:32.280
but the path to reach it does not.

38:32.760 --> 38:34.480
We spend an inordinate amount of time

38:34.480 --> 38:35.940
debating where we want to end up,

38:36.020 --> 38:37.780
and almost zero time analyzing

38:37.780 --> 38:39.520
the physical and logical geometry

38:39.520 --> 38:41.760
of how we are allowed to move.

38:41.860 --> 38:43.300
And that is the true shape of search.

38:43.860 --> 38:45.500
Search in any complex system

38:45.500 --> 38:47.840
is not just about ranking destinations on a map.

38:48.200 --> 38:50.280
It is the active, constrained process

38:50.280 --> 38:51.860
of manufacturing adjacency.

38:52.320 --> 38:53.700
It is the system deciding

38:53.700 --> 38:55.600
what parts of the universe count as next.

38:55.600 --> 38:57.140
To leave you with a final,

38:57.340 --> 38:58.640
slightly provocative thought,

38:59.000 --> 39:00.300
I want to look at a concept

39:00.300 --> 39:02.360
Flyxion references called the clipboard.

39:03.420 --> 39:04.860
We usually think of memory,

39:05.520 --> 39:07.640
whether it is taking notes in a meeting,

39:07.860 --> 39:09.280
saving a file on a hard drive,

39:09.560 --> 39:11.920
or copying a URL to a clipboard

39:11.920 --> 39:13.920
as preserving the past state of something.

39:14.340 --> 39:15.960
We think we're making a pristine,

39:16.180 --> 39:17.840
perfect record of where we were.

39:18.000 --> 39:19.700
A snapshot of the ultimate endpoint.

39:20.100 --> 39:22.080
But based on everything we have discussed today

39:22.080 --> 39:23.840
regarding the continuation structure,

39:23.840 --> 39:26.160
what if our understanding of memory

39:26.160 --> 39:27.620
is entirely backward?

39:28.240 --> 39:30.840
What if the true, functional purpose of a note,

39:31.220 --> 39:32.560
a saved game checkpoint,

39:32.880 --> 39:34.100
or a biological memory

39:34.100 --> 39:36.080
isn't to perfectly record where you were,

39:36.340 --> 39:38.800
but to compress just enough vital information

39:38.800 --> 39:40.160
so that your future self

39:40.160 --> 39:42.480
can reconstruct the paths available to you?

39:42.600 --> 39:43.600
Oh, that is so good.

39:43.920 --> 39:44.880
A memory is valuable

39:44.880 --> 39:47.100
not because it freezes the state in amber,

39:47.520 --> 39:49.360
but because its compression retains

39:49.360 --> 39:51.120
enough of the continuation structure

39:51.120 --> 39:53.400
that a later system can reconstruct

39:53.400 --> 39:55.840
a useful family of future trajectories.

39:56.200 --> 39:57.700
So the next time you write something down

39:57.700 --> 39:59.020
in a journal to remember it,

39:59.360 --> 39:59.860
ask yourself,

39:59.920 --> 40:00.780
are you saving the endpoint,

40:01.200 --> 40:02.420
or are you saving your options?

40:02.680 --> 40:04.420
Are you saving the final score of the game,

40:04.840 --> 40:07.020
or are you saving the playbook?

40:07.440 --> 40:09.080
That is a brilliant place to leave it.

40:09.080 --> 40:12.940
Thank you so much for joining us

40:12.940 --> 40:14.620
on this incredible deep dive.

40:14.900 --> 40:16.640
We covered a massive amount of ground today,

40:16.800 --> 40:18.600
from the sub-nanosecond physics

40:18.600 --> 40:20.080
of magnetic probability,

40:20.420 --> 40:22.800
to the weird distorting observer effects

40:22.800 --> 40:23.580
of human attention,

40:23.980 --> 40:25.260
to the hidden gravity

40:25.260 --> 40:27.500
silently guiding artificial intelligence.

40:28.060 --> 40:29.420
Keep questioning the endpoints

40:29.420 --> 40:30.260
you see every day.

40:30.680 --> 40:31.700
Keep questioning the shape

40:31.700 --> 40:32.980
of the information around you.

40:33.340 --> 40:33.760
And remember,

40:33.900 --> 40:34.860
the score at the end of the game

40:34.860 --> 40:35.840
never actually tells you

40:35.840 --> 40:36.820
how hard it was to play.

40:36.820 --> 41:06.800
Thank you.

